 this hour we'd love to have you  teach physics so thank you very much  thank you very much and congratulations  to all the recipients now is my pleasure  to introduce Provost Robert a brown who  were recognized in 1998-1999  Martin Luther King jr. visiting  professors Provost Brown Thank You Shana  this morning has reminded me of an  extraordinary number of things but I  think it's reminded us all of the  commitment we have to have a building an  academic community that exemplifies the  path that Martin Luther King jr.  challenged us to follow the Martin  Luther King jr. visiting professor  program began in 1994 to bring the MIT  outstanding scholars to participate in  teaching and research at the Institute  and to enrich our community appointments  to this program are made upon  recommendations from the departments and  to the provost this year there have been  five Martin Luther King jr. visiting  professors I'd like to take this  opportunity to recognize the four  individuals who are here this morning  and to introduce you at least through my  words to the fifth I'll ask each one to  stand before at the table where I was  sitting seated and give you a brief  description of them  dr. Lloyd Demetrius  dr. Demetrius is visiting in the  department of brain and cognitive  science he is with the Department of  Biology at Harvard University and is an  expert in mathematics and biophysics in  in the wonderful world of nonlinear  dynamical systems his research at MIT  has spanned across biology chemistry in  brain and cognitive science and is now  taking him into bioengineering and  chemical engineering this spring his  teaching has been in the course  evolutionary psychology in BCS co-taught  with Professor Steven Pinker truly  integrated in the community  let me introduce mr. sterling hunter  mr. hunter is a visiting professor of  management in the Sloan School where his  research is focusing on organizational  design in art the organized  organizational consequences of  information theory and in my team mr.  hunter is also interested in the virtual  organization his teaching has been in  the graduate subject in strategic  management  let me introduce dr. Linda Jordan dr.  Jordan is visiting the Department of  Chemistry she's currently an associate  professor in the Department of Chemistry  at North Carolina Agricultural and  technical State University her research  is in the area of human biochemistry and  expertise in enzymes especially an  enzyme that affects diseases in man  including arthritis asthma pulmonary  disorders and cancer and MIT she's  focused on research along with  undergraduate students on cloning a  specific enzyme in examining the  structural properties of its structural  properties let me introduce dr. Pamela  McCauley Bell  pamela is visiting in the Department of  aeronautics and Astronautics she's  currently an associate professor in  industrial engineering and management  systems in the University of Central  Florida her research isn't focuses on  the development of intelligent software  and predictive models to address  information warfare information security  and human factors problems she's worked  here in the field of human impact of  information security where she's brought  her expertise to a range of groups in  the aero-astro department including the  human factors groups and the group  focused on intelligent systems she's  been teaching a seminar in information  security and she served as the adviser  to seven freshman and two graduate  students while here not with us today is  dr. Arnold Stansell who visited this  fall in the Department of Chemical  Engineering he's a professor currently  at the University of Georgia Institute  of Technology and has returned this  spring to teach there while at MIT he  brought to bear his enormous experience  Arne spent 31 years at mobile before  coming back into the academic profession  he's while here he consulted on energy  issues in the energy lab helped  supervise graduate students and taught  in a core thermodynamics course in  chemical engineering each of these  individuals has enriched the environment  of MIT enormous Lee over their time here  and we look forward to in many cases the  time they have left with us thank you  [Applause]  Thank You Provost Brown for recognizing  our faculty we will now continue with  some announcements that are relevant to  this morning's celebration first there  will be an installation of the Martin  Luther King jr. design project in Lobby  7 at 11 a.m.  Samiha McCord will be performing at noon  in Lobby 7 also there will be a  conference called sisters in cyberspace  youth conference held here at the  Institute on February 6 finally they  will excuse me there fourthly there will  be a presentation than the Black Student  Union which is right through those doors  hosted by the installation committee  from 4:00 to 5:00 p.m. today  finally the South Mass Choir will be  performing in Lobby 7 also at 5:00 p.m.  so I hope you all can attend those  events now would like to introduce  Champlain Betsy Draper who will offer  the benediction for the close of this  morning's program Champlain Draper  everyone stand together please  here we pray O God and father of  humankind forgive our foolish ways may  our renewal today starve the Beast of  racism a favoritism never to devour the  dream of justice for all may our actions  our conduct our words our deeds nourish  the dream and to the reality of harmony  today we ask for courage to serve and to  continue to serve to never give up as  components of change for good in an  ever-changing world we ask for direction  we ask for your sustaining presence as  we rally round the standard of blind  equality leaving behind in the pit our  desires for blind power and pride Oh God  and father of humankind we look forward  to a better day we ask these things amen  well originally we had scheduled for the  MIT gospel choir to sing but now we are  going to move on to just a closing  I hope you may all be seated I mean all  they say that  I hope you all have enjoyed this  morning's program as much as I have and  have taken a moment to reflect and  remember dr. King and the life and the  legacy that he is left behind thank you  all for coming and of course we hope to  see you next year and this concludes the  25th annual Martin Luther King jr.  celebration thank you  [Applause]

